  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 309 AM EST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nThe extended begins Wed night with the main low center moving up the \neastern seaboard becoming centered over western NC by 12z Thu. This \nlow center will continue to push NE and pulling the deep layer \nmoisture with it. Looks like we will still see some showers across \nthe area Thu morning but things will be drying out from south to \nnorth Thanksgiving day. A few light showers will remain possible \nacross far north GA through the late afternoon, but the majority of \nthe state should be dry by early afternoon.  Rainfall totals through \nThursday are forecast to be around 0.5\" across central Georgia and \n0.5\" to 1\" over north Georgia, The movement of the low up the East \nCoast is also expected to displace the high pressure to the north \nand weaken the influence of the wedge over the forecast area. Winds \nwill shift from NE to NW on the back side of this low, with speeds \npicking up Thu afternoon into the 15-20 mph range with gusts 25-30 \nmph. Low temperatures on Thursday will be in the low to mid 40s \nareawide. High temperatures on Thursday will be in the mid to upper \n50s in areas north of I-85 and in the low 60s in areas to the south.\n\nModel guidance is in better agreement on the overall flow pattern \nbehind this system, with cool and dry air overspreading the \nSoutheast late Thursday into Friday followed by surface high \nsettling over the area. The presence of some wraparound moisture in \nfar north Georgia cannot be ruled out on Friday morning on the \nsouthern flank of the mid level low, though this will depend on the \nlow's position as it continues east. Low temperatures on Friday and \nSaturday mornings will range from the upper 20s to mid 30s.",
